integrated_rules:
For any proposed policy change labeled “more gun control,” decide as follows: 

1) Enact additional gun control laws (to reduce gun-related deaths) if you can reasonably establish *all* of these: 
- (Constitutional room) the Second Amendment is not an unlimited individual right to own guns, so regulations aimed at reducing public harm are constitutionally permissible; 
- (Mechanism/evidence) there is credible evidence of a mechanism linking firearms availability to increased risk that conflicts become violent (and thus increased harm), and the proposal specifically targets reducing access in high-risk situations / governs who can buy-possess / requires responsible handling; 
- (Legitimacy/acceptability) the measures are “commonsense” in form and have broad democratic acceptability—e.g., a majority of adults, including gun owners themselves, support similar measures. 

2) Do *not* enact additional gun control laws if you can reasonably establish *all* of these: 
- (Heightened justification needed) the Second Amendment protects an individual right to keep and bear arms, so new restrictions must be especially well-justified; 
- (Operational overbreadth) “more gun control” typically works by imposing categorical limits on lawful ownership (who can possess, where they can carry, and under what broad conditions) without focusing on individualized proof of illegal conduct or disqualifying behavior, thereby burdening law-abiding citizens even when there is no case-specific determination they pose an identifiable threat; 
- (Better alternative) stopping illegal firearm access and criminal violence would be better achieved by enforcing existing prohibitions using ordinary criminal-justice tools (investigation, prosecution, and due-process adjudication against unlawful actors) rather than expanding statutory restrictions that sweep in lawful owners. 

3) If the argument for enactment meets some but not all of (1)’s conditions, and/or the argument against enactment meets some but not all of (2)’s conditions, then the rule does not determine the outcome (leave it unresolved rather than automatically choosing either side).
